  "architecturalPlan": {
    "scaleRationale": "The US$25m figure remains an assumed-USD ambition reference rather than a construction-cost, valuation, or floor-area formula. This unapproved revision preserves the estate-scale salon, private pavilions, owner suite, water court, and lounge while correcting the upper-level geometry rather than enlarging the compound. The flat Y=0 site remains a temporary modeled condition, not a survey, flood datum, or coastal approval.",
    "arrivalAndCirculation": "Public arrival remains through arrival-walk and the sheltered salon threshold; private family and guest pavilions retain independent court-facing entries. The owner suite now has a new east-side door reached by an explicit, exterior eighteen-tread stair, upper landing, supported north-south walk, and bridge at the upper walking datum. Assumptions: the reconstructed stair is intentionally exterior to avoid cutting the salon’s occupied slab and roof, with 0.206 m modeled rises from water-terrace to owner-stair-18; constructed IDs are owner-stair-01 through owner-stair-18, owner-stair-landing, owner-stair-walk, owner-stair-bridge, owner-stair-post-a, owner-stair-post-b, upper-east, and upper-east-door. The rectangular pool remains a dry-deck assembly with basin perimeter, bottom, coping, and water only; no hydraulic, filtration, excavation, or drainage decision is represented.",
    "volumeHierarchy": "Two possible corrections were considered: a central roof opening with an internal stair through the salon, or an exterior east-side ascent with roofs restricted to the salon’s flanking bays. The selected concept, “Water Court Promenade—Flanking Roof Edition,” retains the salon as the primary low datum and makes the owner suite a visibly independent lantern above an intentional roof void. The side pavilions, pool lounge, portal canopy, and new circulation bridge remain subordinate to that main section.",
    "relationshipsAndViews": "The salon continues to open toward water-terrace and the pool court, while the suite reaches its new east door from an external bridge and retains its broad view opening. owner-stair-01 starts from a new narrow extension of the existing terrace, then rises clear of the salon envelope at the east side before turning to the bridge. The pool lounge remains beside the dry pool circuit, with its access still dependent on the adjoining pool deck geometry rather than a modeled door.",
    "roofFacadeAndMaterials": "The conflict is resolved by removing salon-roof and replacing it with salon-roof-west and salon-roof-east, two flat, deep soffit roof fields that stop 0.2 m clear of the owner-suite footprint; the upper suite therefore has no lower roof passing through its floor or enclosure. family-wing-roof is narrowed to terminate at the salon edge, eliminating its plan overlap into the salon. Ivory enclosure, limestone floors and stair solids, bronze upper entry, and timber screens continue the tropical palette; facade assumptions are that deep eaves and screens express shade intent only, with drainage, waterproofing, hurricane, corrosion, and thermal performance unverified.",
    "intendedSpaces": [
      "Great salon, dining and kitchen",
      "Family bedroom pavilion",
      "Guest bedroom pavilion",
      "Upper owner suite",
      "Pool terrace and water court",
      "Open pool lounge",
      "Arrival threshold",
      "Owner stair, bridge and upper landing"
    ],
